USAGE: oss [-h] [-n #] [-s #] [-t #]\n"
[-h]: Throws help message: No execution.\n");
[-n]: expects integer value for max total children to launch\n");
[-s]: expects integer value for number of max simultaneous children\n");
[-t]: expects integer value for iterations in child loop\n");

EXAMPLEs:

./oss -n 12 -s 3 -t 4
./oss -s 5 
./oss -n 7 -t 4
./oss -h

COMPILE:
	Compile using make utiliy within directory containing oss.c, 
	user.c, and makefile.

RUN:
	After compiling, run program using './oss' execution with 
	appropriate usage as above.

DESCRIPTION:
	OSS program will create a number of child processes, up to S 
	simultaneous children,
	until it has created N total children. 

	The child process, USER, will iterate through a loop T number 
	of times, with intermediate sleeping, then close.

	Upon USER closing, OSS will continue to launch a number of
	chilren, up to S simultaneous, until N children is reached.

OUTSTANDING ISSUES:
	The program operates as intended. Many learning opportunities
	during coding, many minor challenges overcome. The only 
	persisting issue is that my EXECVP call will not execute
	UNLESS a printf statement came before it(?). I have no idea
	why and would love to know what the link between the two are.
	This print statement is commented similarly within OSS.C.
